{{also|Lump}}
==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|lumpe}}, from a {{der|en|gem|-}} base akin to {{der|en|gem-pro|*limpaną|t=to glide, go, hang loosely}}. Compare {{cog|nl|lomp|t=rag}}, {{cog|nds-de|Lump|t=rag}}, {{cog|de|Lumpen|t=rag}} and {{m|de|Lump|t=ragamuffin}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/lʌmp/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-lump.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ʌmp|s=1}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# Something that [[protrudes]], sticks out, or sticks together; a [[cluster]] or [[blob]]; a [[mound]] or [[mass]] of no particular [[shape]].
#: ''Stir the gravy until there are no more '''lumps'''.''
#: ''a '''lump''' of coal; a '''lump''' of clay; a '''lump''' of cheese''
# A [[swelling]] or [[nodule]] of [[tissue]] under the [[skin]] or in an [[internal]] part of the [[body]].
# A [[group]], [[set]], or [[unit]].
#: ''The money arrived all at once as one big '''lump''' sum payment.''
# A small, shaped mass of [[sugar]], typically about a [[teaspoonful]].
#: ''Do you want one '''lump''' or two with your coffee?''
# A [[dull]] or [[lazy]] [[person]].
#: ''Don't just sit there like a '''lump'''.''
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1971|author=Richard Carpenter|authorlink=Richard Carpenter (screenwriter)|title=Catweazle and the Magic Zodiac|publisher=Puffin Books|location=Harmondsworth|page=7|text="Thou great Norman '''lump'''!" he muttered. "If I conjure till Doomsday, I cannot make thee gold."}}
# {{lb|en|informal|as plural}} A [[beating]] or [[verbal]] [[abuse]].
#: ''He's taken his '''lumps''' over the years.''
#* {{quote-book|en|title=The cold war on the periphery: the United States, India, and Pakistan|page=323|author=Robert J. McMahon|year=1994|passage=Komer admitted that the United States would probably suffer "short term '''lumps'''" as a result of Johnson's brusque decision.}}
# A [[projection]] beneath the [[breech]] end of a [[gun]] [[barrel]].
# A kind of [[fish]], the [[lumpsucker]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1863|author=Sheridan Le Fanu|title=The House by the Churchyard|passage=You roast him [the fish] {{...}} just like a '''lump'''.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete|slang}} [[food|Food]] given to a [[tramp]] to be eaten [[on the road]].
#* '''1923''', Arthur Preston Hankins, ''Cole of Spyglass Mountain,'' New York: Grosset & Dunlap, Chapter{{nbsp}}12,<sup>[https://www.fadedpage.com/books/20150532/html.php]</sup>
#*: “A '''lump''',” explained The Whimperer {{...}} “is wot a kin’ lady slips youse w’en youse batter de back door. If she invites youse in and lets youse t’row yer feet unner de table, it’s a [[set-down]]. If she slips youse a lunch in a poiper bag, it’s a '''lump'''. See? {{...}}”

